    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Hongshan culture</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 6,700 years ago, the Hongshan people lived in northeast China, in lands from Inner Mongolia to Liaoning.</p><p>Back then their land had deep lakes and forests, not the desert it is today.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Millet <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kurt Stüber [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They were farmers who grew millet, a small grain.</p><p>In later times, millet made up most of what they ate.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yellow-river.jpg" alt="Yellow River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Yellow River <span class="kid-tale-cr">fading, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They were in touch with farmers by the Yellow River, who passed on stone knives for harvesting.</p><p>Some farmers from farther south moved north and became part of the Hongshan people.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>At Niuheliang they built an underground temple with painted walls and clay figures up to three times as big as people.</p><p>A clay head with eyes of jade gave it the name Goddess Temple.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They built tombs of stone, covered them with mounds of stones and often placed jade objects inside.</p><p>Their jade pig dragons are some of the earliest jade carvings known, and small copper rings have been found too.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liao-river.jpg" alt="Liao River" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Liao River <span class="kid-tale-cr">吴张帆, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Hongshan culture ended about 4,900 years ago, and later a changing climate turned the lakes and forests into desert.</p><p>Their jade and tombs remain, and they are believed to have helped shape early Chinese civilisation.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Hongshan culture farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> in the West Liao river basin of northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from about 4700 to 2900 BCE; in its late phase millet made up as much as 80% of the diet.</li><li>Its burials hold some of the earliest known examples of jade working, including the jade pig dragons and embryo dragons for which the culture is known.</li><li>At Niuheliang it built a ritual complex of stone cairn tombs and an underground “Goddess Temple” holding clay figures up to three times life size.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The “Goddess Temple” at Niuheliang takes its name from a clay female head with eyes inlaid with jade, found among clay figures as large as three times the size of living people.</p></aside>
    </section>

